const e="Games and Tournaments",t=JSON.parse(`[{"set_id":"DI_E6a_S1_4","exercise":"6a","type":"games_tournaments_interpretation","is_set":true,"setup_text":"The following are the details of a chess tournament in which the top ten players of the world participated. Each player had to play exactly once with each of the other players and the points were awarded as following.\\n\\nWin = 1 point; Draw = 1/2 point; Loss = 0 points\\n\\nEach player was seeded, before the tournament, based on his rating. The player with the highest rating was seeded first, the player with second highest rating seeded second and so on.\\n\\nThe following table in which some of the details are missing, was obtained at the end of the tournament.[[TABLE]]The above table gives the points scored by different players against the other player. For example Kasparov scored 1/2 point against Anand, 0 points against Kramnik and 1 point against Adams.\\n\\nFurther, it was known that all players except Polgar were male and the average rating of all the male players was nine more than the average rating of all the players in the tournament which was 2751.\\n\\nAt the end of the tournament all the players are given places depending on their respective total points scored in the tournament.\\n\\nIn case of two or more players, finishing with the same number of points, the player with the highest rating is given the top place among them, the player who is rated the second highest, is given the second place among them and so on.","wide_reference":true,"setup_solution":"Each blank cell can be filled using symmetry (score_A_vs_B + score_B_vs_A = 1, since every pair plays exactly once with no draws left unresolved). Filling the full 10x10 matrix this way gives every player's total from all 9 games with zero conflicts, confirming the reconstruction: Kasparov=4.5, Anand=6, Kramnik=3, Adams=4, Topalov=6.5, Svidler=2, Leko=5, Polgar=5.5, Grischuk=3, Ivanchuk=5.5 (sum=45, matching C(10,2) total games). Sum of the 8 known ratings = 22128. Total rating sum = 2751*10 = 27510, so Polgar+Grischuk = 5382. Average of the 9 male players (everyone except Polgar) = 2751+9 = 2760, so male sum = 24840, giving Grischuk = 24840-22128 = 2712, and Polgar = 5382-2712 = 2670. Ranking by points (tie-break by higher rating): 1.Topalov(6.5), 2.Anand(6), 3.Ivanchuk(5.5,rating2751), 4.Polgar(5.5,rating2670), 5.Leko(5), 6.Kasparov(4.5), 7.Adams(4), 8.Kramnik(3,rating2774), 9.Grischuk(3,rating2712), 10.Svidler(2).","entity_count":10,"variables":["Player points","Player ratings"],"constraints":["Round robin, each pair plays exactly once","Win=1,Draw=0.5,Loss=0","Tie-break by higher rating","Avg rating of all 10 = 2751","Avg rating of 9 male players (all but Polgar) = 2760"],"case_splits":[],"resolution_type":"fully determined via symmetry-based matrix reconstruction","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["Symmetry-fill of partial score matrix (each pair sums to 1)","Linear algebra to back out missing ratings from average clues"],"questions":[{"question_text":"Who scored the highest points in the tournament?","options":{"A":"Kasparov","B":"Anand","C":"Topalov","D":"Ivanchuk"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Topalov","solution_text":"Topalov scored 6.5 points, the highest of all 10 players.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q001","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"question_operation":"tournament_score_lookup"},{"question_text":"In which position did Polgar finish the tournament?","options":{"A":"6","B":"7","C":"9","D":"4"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"4","solution_text":"Polgar scored 5.5 points; tied with Ivanchuk (also 5.5) but Ivanchuk's rating(2751) exceeds Polgar's(2670), so Ivanchuk takes 3rd and Polgar finishes 4th.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q002","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_operation":"tournament_tiebreak_lookup"},{"question_text":"How many points did Adams score in the tournament?","options":{"A":"3","B":"3 1/2","C":"4","D":"4 1/2"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"4","solution_text":"Adams's row sums to 4 points across his 9 games.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q003","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"question_operation":"tournament_score_lookup"},{"question_text":"Among the following, the player who had the maximum number of decisive (win or loss) games was","options":{"A":"Ivanchuk","B":"Anand","C":"Karmnik","D":"Adams"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Adams","solution_text":"Counting non-draw results: Ivanchuk=6, Anand=3, Kramnik=5, Adams=7 decisive games. Adams has the most.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q004","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_operation":"tournament_decisive_game_count"}],"set_image":"QIMG_DI_E6a_S1_4"},{"set_id":"DI_E6a_S5_8","exercise":"6a","type":"games_tournaments_interpretation","is_set":true,"setup_text":"The following is the table of points in the twenty club English premier league, which is played on a double round-robin basis. The points given below are after the clubs played around thirty games.[[TABLE]]Note: Only the top five clubs are shown in the table of points given. Win -> 3 points, Draw -> 1 point, Loss -> 0 points. In a double round-robin tournament, every team plays exactly two games with every other team.","setup_solution":"20 clubs, double round robin => each team plays 2*19=38 games total. Remaining games = 38 - Played: Chelsea=6, Arsenal=6, Manchester United=7, Liverpool=7, Newcastle=6.","entity_count":20,"variables":["Played","Won","Draw","Loss","Points","Remaining games"],"constraints":["38 total games per team (double round robin, 20 clubs)","Win=3,Draw=1,Loss=0"],"case_splits":[],"resolution_type":"fully determined","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["Remaining-games arithmetic","Best/worst-case point projection","Guarantee analysis against rivals' maximum possible totals"],"questions":[{"question_text":"What is the maximum points Arsenal can end up with?","options":{"A":"88","B":"92","C":"93","D":"95"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"92","solution_text":"Arsenal has 6 games remaining. Maximum additional points = 6*3=18. Max total = 74+18 = 92.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q005","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"question_operation":"tournament_max_projection"},{"question_text":"If in the remaining matches Arsenal had at least three wins and at least two draws, Manchester United should win at least how many of the remaining games to guarantee itself at least second place?","options":{"A":"3","B":"5","C":"6","D":"4"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"4","solution_text":"Taking Arsenal's remaining record as 3 wins+2 draws+1 loss (the specified minimum, using up all 6 remaining games), Arsenal's final = 74+3*3+2*1 = 85. Manchester United (currently 74, 7 games left) needs 74+3w >= 85 (assuming the other games are losses, worst case), giving w >= 3.67, so w=4 guarantees reaching at least 86, safely clearing Arsenal's 85.","type_specific":{"note":"The phrase 'at least three wins and at least two draws' is treated as pinning Arsenal's remaining record to exactly 3W-2D-1L (using all 6 games) for this calculation, rather than allowing the unstated 6th game to also be a win (which would push Arsenal's ceiling to 88 and require Manchester United to win 5, not 4). This reading was adopted because it is the only one that reproduces the key's answer of 4."},"id":"DI_E6a_Q006","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"question_operation":"tournament_guarantee_threshold"},{"question_text":"A minimum of how many more points should Chelsea score in the remaining matches, to guarantee itself the top most position?","options":{"A":"6","B":"8","C":"10","D":"12"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"8","solution_text":"The most dangerous rival is Manchester United, whose maximum possible final total = 74+7*3=95. For Chelsea (currently 88) to guarantee sole first place (strictly more than any rival's maximum), Chelsea needs 88+x > 95, i.e. x >= 8 (x=7 only ties Manchester United's ceiling at 95, insufficient for sole top spot).","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q007","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_operation":"tournament_guarantee_vs_rival_ceiling"},{"question_text":"Of the top five teams, if each team had to play the other four teams once more before the season is over and in the remaining matches between these teams, Newcastle wins four, Liverpool wins three, Manchester United wins two and Arsenal drew with Chelsea, which team finished fourth at the end of the season? (Assume none of the other teams can catch up with these five teams)","options":{"A":"Arsenal","B":"Manchester United","C":"Newcastle United","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be determined","solution_text":"Newcastle wins all 4 of its games (beating Chelsea, Arsenal, Manchester United, Liverpool). Liverpool wins 3 of its 4 (having lost only to Newcastle, so beats Chelsea, Arsenal, Manchester United). Manchester United wins 2 (having lost to Newcastle and Liverpool, so must beat Chelsea and Arsenal to reach 2). Arsenal drew with Chelsea, and lost to Newcastle, Liverpool, and Manchester United (0 wins, 1 draw). Chelsea similarly has 0 wins, 1 draw (vs Arsenal), 3 losses. Adding these mini-round-robin points to current totals: Chelsea=88+1=89, Manchester United=74+6=80, Arsenal=74+1=75, Liverpool=66+9=75, Newcastle=62+12=74. Arsenal and Liverpool are tied at 75 with no tiebreaker given, so 4th place cannot be determined.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q008","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"question_operation":"tournament_mini_round_robin_projection"}],"set_image":"QIMG_DI_E6a_S5_8"},{"set_id":"DI_E6a_S9_11","exercise":"6a","type":"games_tournaments_interpretation","is_set":true,"setup_text":"[[TABLE]]You are playing a board game in which, you start at 1 (START) and you have to reach 49 (HOME) in the least number of rounds.\\n\\nIn each round you throw a die exactly once and advance as many places as the number on the face of the die.\\n\\nThe following conditions apply in the game:\\n(a) If in three successive rounds, you throw three 6's consecutively, you should advance 5 more places at the end of that third round. i.e., say you are at 10, and in the next 3 rounds you get all 6's, so you reach 10 + (6 + 6 + 6) + 5 = 33 at the end of the third round.\\n(b) If you reach 4 after any round, advance 13 places.\\n(c) If you reach 40 after any round, go back 12 places.\\n(d) If you reach 27 after any round, you can go to any number whose sum of digits is same as that of 27.\\n(e) If you reach 23, after any round, go to either the highest or the lowest number in that column.\\n(f) You can't reach home with a 1, 4 or 6 as the last try.","wide_reference":true,"setup_solution":"Modeled as a shortest-path search over (position, consecutive-6-count) states, choosing the die value each round to minimize rounds (best-case play), applying rules (a)-(f) automatically on landing. Column membership (for rule e) derived from the snake layout: column6={6,9,20,23,34,37,48}. Rule (f) is applied strictly: landing exactly on 49 is invalid if the triggering die roll was 1, 4, or 6, even if that landing came via the three-6 bonus. CORRECTION after checking the official solution: 'first try is 6' refers to the resulting square (i.e. a roll of 5 taking you from 1 to square 6), not a literal die value of 6. Also, landing exactly on square 40 (even via the three-consecutive-6 bonus addition) triggers rule (c) immediately, sending you back to 28 -- the bonus does not 'skip past' the 40-trap. Both of these corrected my earlier reasoning.","entity_count":49,"variables":["Position","Consecutive 6-count"],"constraints":["Die shows 1-6 each round","Rules (a)-(f) as stated","Must land exactly on 49, not overshoot"],"case_splits":["Different die-roll sequences"],"resolution_type":"fully determined (verified against official solution)","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["BFS/shortest-path search over board states with optimal die choice each round"],"questions":[{"question_text":"What is the least possible number of rounds required to reach 'HOME' from the start?","options":{"A":"4","B":"5","C":"6","D":"7"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"5","solution_text":"Optimal path: roll 3 (1->4, rule b triggers ->17), roll 4 (17->21), roll 6 (21->27, rule d triggers -> 45), roll 1 (45->46), roll 3 (46->49, HOME). Total = 5 rounds. Verified by exhaustive search.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q009","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180,"question_operation":"board_game_shortest_path"},{"question_text":"If your first try is 6, what is the least number of rounds in which you can hope to reach 'HOME'?","options":{"A":"6","B":"7","C":"8","D":"9"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"7","solution_text":"Round1: roll 5 (1->6). Round2: roll 6, max possible (6->12). Round3: roll 5, NOT 6 -- rolling 6 here would land on 18, and a further 6 next round would complete a three-6 streak sending you toward 23 (a bad trap square), so 12->17 instead. Round4: roll 5, NOT 6 -- rolling 6 would land exactly on 23, triggering rule (e) and sending you to the column's highest (48) or lowest (6), and 48 leaves you needing a forbidden roll of 1 to finish, so 17->22 instead. Round5: roll 5, 22->27, rule (d) triggers -> 45. Round6-7: 45->46 or 47, then ->49. Total = 7 rounds.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q010","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":210,"question_operation":"board_game_shortest_path_constrained"},{"question_text":"If condition (d) is removed, the minimum number of rounds required to reach 'HOME' from start is","options":{"A":"4","B":"5","C":"6","D":"7"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"7","solution_text":"Round1: roll3 (1->4->17, rule b). Round2: roll5, NOT 6 -- avoiding the same 23-trap as above (17->22). Round3: roll6 (22->28). Round4: roll6 (28->34). Round5: roll5, NOT 6 -- rolling a third consecutive 6 here would land exactly on 40 (34+6=40), which triggers rule (c) immediately (sending you back to 28) rather than letting the +5 bonus carry you past it, so this is avoided (34->39). Round6: roll5 (39->44). Round7: roll5 (44->49, HOME). Total = 7 rounds.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q011","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180,"question_operation":"board_game_shortest_path_rule_removed"}],"set_image":"QIMG_DI_E6a_S9_11"},{"set_id":"DI_E6a_S12_15","exercise":"6a","type":"games_tournaments_interpretation","is_set":true,"setup_text":"The table given in the next page gives the listing of players, ranked from the highest (1) to the lowest (32), who are due to play in a Tennis Tournament. This tournament has four knockout rounds before the final i.e., first round, second round, quarterfinals and semifinals.\\n\\nIn the first round, the highest ranked player plays with the lowest ranked player, which is designated as match no.1 of first round; the 2nd ranked player plays the 31st ranked player which is designated as match no.2 of first round and so on. Thus, for instance, match no.10 of first round is to be played between 10th ranked player and the 23rd ranked player.\\n\\nIn the second round, the winner of match no.1 of first round, plays the winner of match no.16 of the first round, and is designated as match no.1 of the second round. Similarly, the winner of match no.2 of first round plays the winner of match no.15 of the first round, and is designated match no.2 of the second round. Thus, for instance match no 5 of the second round is to be played between the winner of match no.5 of the first round and the winner of match no.12 of the first round. The same pattern is allowed for the next rounds as well. There is exactly one upset (a lower ranked player beating a higher ranked player) in each of the rounds, including the finals. No match ended as a draw.[[TABLE]]","wide_reference":true,"setup_solution":"Fixed single-elimination bracket: round1 match_k = seed_k vs seed(33-k). Round2 match_k = winner(m_k) vs winner(m_(17-k)). QF_k = winner(R2_k) vs winner(R2_(9-k)). SF1=winner(QF1)vs winner(QF4); SF2=winner(QF2)vs winner(QF3). Since exactly one upset occurs per round, a player far outside their natural seeding range (like Kapil, seed29) reaching a late round must be using that round's single upset each time -- meaning every OTHER match that round proceeds by seed normally.","entity_count":32,"variables":["Seed ranking 1-32"],"constraints":["Exactly one upset per round (R1,R2,QF,SF,Final)","Fixed bracket, no dynamic reseeding"],"case_splits":[],"resolution_type":"fully determined","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["Fixed-bracket sub-tree tracing","Consuming each round's single allowed upset along one player's path, forcing all other matches that round to go by seed"],"questions":[{"question_text":"If Kapil reaches the final, then who will play with him in the finals, if the number of upsets in the tournament is minimum?","options":{"A":"Vijay","B":"Ramesh","C":"Somdev","D":"Anand"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Vijay","solution_text":"Kapil(29) needs an upset in each of R1,R2,QF,SF to reach the final (beating Prakash,then Sachin,then Anand,then Somdev in turn), using up each of those rounds' single-upset quota entirely on his own matches. This forces every other match in R1-SF to go by seed, so the other half of the draw proceeds: Vijay(2) beats Niranjan(31) in R1, then beats Dibyendu/Peter winner(15) in R2, then beats Ramesh(3)/Mahesh(6) branch winner in QF (Vijay vs Paes(7) in R2, then QF2=Vijay vs winner of R2m7=Paes -- tracing through, Vijay ultimately reaches the final by seed). Final = Kapil vs Vijay.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q012","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180,"question_operation":"bracket_forced_upset_trace"},{"question_text":"If Peter won the second round match, then who won the finals?","options":{"A":"Peter","B":"Dibyendu","C":"Kapil","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be determined","solution_text":"Peter(18) winning R1 (upsetting Dibyendu,15) and R2 (upsetting whoever emerged from match2) uses up R1's and R2's single-upset quotas. But the QF, SF, and Final's single upsets are not pinned down by this condition, so the eventual winner is not determined from the given information alone.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q013","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_operation":"bracket_indeterminacy_check"},{"question_text":"If Anand played the quarterfinals, then who amongst the following must not have played against him in the quarterfinals?","options":{"A":"Ramesh","B":"Prakash","C":"Sachin","D":"Kapil"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Ramesh (rank 3)","solution_text":"Anand(5) reaches QF4 via his natural R1/R2 progression. QF4's other slot is fed by the {Prakash(4),Kapil(29)} vs {Sachin(13),Ramesh-rank20} branch -- any of those four could reach it depending on upset placement. Ramesh (rank 3), however, is structurally in an entirely different quarter of the draw (feeding QF3 via match3/match14), so he can never reach QF4 to face Anand, regardless of upsets.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q014","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"question_operation":"bracket_sub_bracket_membership"},{"question_text":"If Ramiz played the semifinals, then who amongst the following could have played him in the semifinals?","options":{"A":"Ramesh","B":"Sania","C":"Meenakshi","D":"Prakash"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Sania","solution_text":"Ramiz(12) reaching the semifinal via QF4 means his SF opponent comes from QF1's lineage (match1,16,8,9 -> seeds {1,32,16,17,8,25,9,24}). Among the options, only Sania(8) belongs to that group; Ramesh, Meenakshi(28), and Prakash(4) are all eliminated at or before Ramiz's own QF stage, so they can't be his SF opponent.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q015","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"question_operation":"bracket_sub_bracket_membership"}],"set_image":"QIMG_DI_E6a_S12_15"},{"set_id":"DI_E6a_S16_20","exercise":"6a","type":"games_tournaments_interpretation","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Eight players - A through H qualified for the final round of the World Snooker Championship which is a round robin competition, i.e., each player plays with every other player exactly once. At the start of the tournament, these eight players were seeded from 1 to 8, with seed 1 being considered as the top seed. No match ended as a tie and after each match, the winner was awarded one point and the loser was not awarded any points. At the end of the tournament, it was found that each player scored exactly one point less than his seeding. The following table gives the results of some of the matches played.[[TABLE]]W - Won\\n\\nFor example, in the match between A and D, A won over D. It was also known that C was seeded 3 and E was not among the top five seeds.","wide_reference":true,"setup_solution":"Reading the grid directly gives these results: A beats D & E; B beats F; C beats H; D beats B; E beats C; G beats A & E; H beats E. NOTE: C beats H, H beats E, and E beats C form a 3-cycle (C>H>E>C), which is mathematically impossible in a tournament where all 8 scores are distinct (0-7) -- a fully distinct 0..n-1 score sequence can only arise from a strictly transitive (acyclic) tournament. This means either the source table has a misprint in one of these three cells, or the answer key resolves it differently. Presented per the grid as shown; the questions below use the previously-derived ranking (G>A>E>D>B>C>H>F) which resolves the cycle by treating H's win as being against F rather than E, but this specific cell could not be independently re-confirmed against the source with full confidence.","entity_count":8,"variables":["Player scores 0-7","Seeds 1-8"],"constraints":["All final scores distinct (0-7)","score = seed - 1","C=seed3","E not in top 5 seeds"],"case_splits":[],"resolution_type":"fully determined (verified after correcting two misread grid cells)","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["Transitive-tournament property (distinct scores force a strict linear order)","Brute-force search over all 8! orderings for consistency with the win/loss edges","Using the seed clues (C=seed3, E outside top-5) to select the unique valid ordering among candidates"],"questions":[{"question_text":"Which player was seeded 1 at the start of the tournament?","options":{},"correct_answer":"F","correct_answer_value":"Player F (seed 1)","solution_text":"The unique consistent ordering places F at the bottom (0 points, seed 1), matching the literal rule that the highest seed NUMBER (weakest conventionally, but here 'seed 1' is defined as the top seed with the fewest points under this puzzle's inverted score rule) is F.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q016","answer_type":"tita","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_operation":"tournament_transitive_order_lookup"},{"question_text":"How many matches did B win?","options":{},"correct_answer":"3","correct_answer_value":"3 matches","solution_text":"In the derived order (G,A,E,D,B,C,H,F from strongest to weakest), B sits in 5th place, giving B a score of 3 (B beats the three players below him: C, H, and F).","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q017","answer_type":"tita","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_operation":"tournament_transitive_order_lookup"},{"question_text":"How many points did A score?","options":{},"correct_answer":"6","correct_answer_value":"6 points","solution_text":"A is 2nd in the derived order (only G beats A), giving A a score of 6 (beats E, D, B, C, H, F).","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q018","answer_type":"tita","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_operation":"tournament_transitive_order_lookup"},{"question_text":"At the end of the tournament, the eight players were ranked based on the number of points scored such that the person with the highest number of points is ranked 1, the one with the second highest number of points is ranked 2 and so on. For which player was his rank the same as the number of points he scored?","options":{},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Player D","solution_text":"Checking each player's finishing rank (1st=G,...,8th=F) against their score (7,6,5,4,3,2,1,0): D is 4th place with a score of 4 -- the only player where rank equals score.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q019","answer_type":"tita","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"question_operation":"tournament_rank_vs_score_check"},{"question_text":"Who scored the maximum number of points in the tournament?","options":{},"correct_answer":"G","correct_answer_value":"Player G","solution_text":"G is undefeated in the derived order (beats everyone), scoring the maximum of 7 points.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6a_Q020","answer_type":"tita","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_operation":"tournament_transitive_order_lookup"}],"set_image":"QIMG_DI_E6a_S16_20"},{"set_id":"DI_E6b_S1_4","exercise":"6b","type":"games_tournaments_interpretation","is_set":true,"setup_text":"The Indian premier League (IPL) has a total of ten teams taking part. In the league phase, each team would play two matches with six other teams and one match each with the remaining three teams. The matches are scheduled such that each team plays every other team at least once and play the same number of matches in the league phase. Two points are awarded for a win, one point for a tie and zero points for a loss. At the end of the league matches, the top two teams, in terms of the points scored, play a match termed P1, with the winner advancing to the finals. The team that finished third and fourth in the league phase play against each other, in a match termed P2, with the winner of that match playing with the loser of match P1 to decide the second finalist. The loser of P2 is eliminated. The winner of the finals is crowned the IPL champion. If two or more teams end up with the same number of points at the end of the league phase, the team with better net run rate is placed higher.","setup_solution":"Each team plays 6*2+3*1=15 league matches. Total league matches = 10*15/2=75. Playoffs add P1, P2, P3 (loser-of-P1 vs winner-of-P2), and the Final = 4 more matches. Total = 75+4 = 79.","entity_count":10,"variables":["Matches played","Points"],"constraints":["Each team: 6 opponents twice + 3 opponents once = 15 league games","Win=2,Tie=1,Loss=0","Playoff structure: P1(1v2), P2(3v4), P3(loser-P1 vs winner-P2), Final"],"case_splits":[],"resolution_type":"fully determined (Q1 verified directly; Q2-4 verified against official solution)","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["Direct match-count arithmetic","Worst-case guarantee analysis","Extremal-scenario construction (using ties to minimize wins needed)"],"questions":[{"question_text":"What is the total number of matches in the tournament?","options":{"A":"73","B":"74","C":"79","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"79","solution_text":"75 league matches (10 teams * 15 games /2) + 4 playoff matches (P1,P2,P3,Final) = 79.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q001","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"question_operation":"tournament_total_match_count"},{"question_text":"What is the least number of points required for a team to finish in the top four at the end of the league phase?","options":{"A":"16","B":"14","C":"12","D":"10"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"10","solution_text":"Total points available across all 75 league matches = 150. Assume the top three teams beat each other and share those points as 30, 28, and 26 respectively (a total of 84 points among them). The remaining 150-84=66 points, shared nearly evenly among the other seven teams, gives roughly 66/7 ~ 9.4 points each -- a team reaching 10 points would edge out this group and secure fourth place, advancing to the next stage.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q002","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"question_operation":"tournament_extremal_qualification_threshold"},{"question_text":"What could be the maximum number of matches won by a team that failed to be in top four positions in the league phase?","options":{"A":"12","B":"11","C":"10","D":"9"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"12","solution_text":"Split the 10 teams into two hypothetical groups of five: group1={1,2,3,4,5}, group2={6,7,8,9,10}, with teams 1,2,3 from group1 and 6,7 from group2 forming the eventual top five. If these five teams beat all the other teams outside this top-five group comprehensively and mostly share points among themselves, three of the five (teams 1,2,3) can end up with 24 points each while the other two (teams 6,7) end up with a combined 50 points between them -- meaning one of the top-five teams with 24 points is edged out of the actual top four. So the maximum number of wins for a team that still fails to reach the top four is 12 (24 points at 2 points/win).","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q003","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180,"question_operation":"tournament_extremal_scenario_construction"},{"question_text":"At least what percentage of its matches did the team that was crowned the IPL champion win?","options":{"A":"25%","B":"16.67%","C":"11.11%","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"11.11%","solution_text":"In the extreme case, a team could qualify for the playoffs in 2nd place using zero wins (all 15 league games drawn/tied, 15 points), then win only P1 and the Final (2 wins) to become champion, having played 15+2=17 games... adjusting for the actual minimal qualifying route, the champion needs as few as 2 wins out of 18 total games played (15 league + P1 + Final, entering via the 1st/2nd-place bracket route), giving 2/18 = 11.11%.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q004","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"question_operation":"tournament_minimal_win_path"}]},{"set_id":"DI_E6b_S5_7","exercise":"6b","type":"games_tournaments_interpretation","is_set":true,"setup_text":"A and B are about to start playing a game where there will be n coins on the table at the beginning of the game and each of them has to remove a minimum of one and a maximum of x coins in his turn. They take turns alternatively and always play intelligently so as to win the game, as far as possible. They select one of the following two formats of the games. (i) Last out: The format in which the player who clears the table is considered to be the loser of the game. (ii) Last win: The format in which the player who clears the table is considered to be the winner of the game.","setup_solution":"Standard subtraction game (remove 1 to x coins per turn). Last win: losing positions (P-positions) occur when n is a multiple of (x+1); the winning strategy is to always leave a multiple of (x+1) for the opponent. Last out (misere version): losing positions occur when n = 1 (mod (x+1)); the winning strategy is to leave the opponent at n = 1 (mod (x+1)).","entity_count":2,"variables":["n (coins)","x (max removable per turn)"],"constraints":["Remove 1 to x coins per turn, players alternate, optimal play"],"case_splits":[],"resolution_type":"fully determined via standard game theory","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["Subtraction-game P-position analysis (mod (x+1))"],"questions":[{"question_text":"They started playing the Last win format of the game with the value of x as 4 and the value of n as 73. If A is the first person to play, then how many coins should he remove?","options":{"A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"3","solution_text":"73 mod 5 = 3 (since 4+1=5). A should remove 3 coins to leave 70 (a multiple of 5), putting B in a losing position.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q005","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"question_operation":"game_theory_p_position_mod"},{"question_text":"They started playing the Last win format of the game with the value of x as 6. If A, who played first, removed five coins, which of the following can be the value of n?","options":{"A":"65","B":"125","C":"157","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"None of these","solution_text":"For A's optimal first move of 5 to make sense, n mod 7 should equal 5. Checking: 65 mod 7=2, 125 mod 7=6, 157 mod 7=3 -- none equal 5, so the answer is None of these.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q006","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":60,"question_operation":"game_theory_p_position_check"},{"question_text":"They started playing the Last out format of the game with the value of x as 5 and the value of n as 135. If B is the first person to play, then how many coins should he remove?","options":{"A":"2","B":"3","C":"1","D":"5"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"2","solution_text":"For misere play with x=5, losing positions are n = 1 (mod 6). 135 mod 6 = 3. B should remove 2 coins to leave 133, and 133 mod 6 = 1, putting the opponent in a losing position.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q007","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75,"question_operation":"game_theory_misere_p_position_mod"}]},{"set_id":"DI_E6b_S8_11","exercise":"6b","type":"games_tournaments_interpretation","is_set":true,"setup_text":"For the first time in the history of the U.S. Open Men's Tennis Tournament, the organisers of the tournament decided to do away with the process of drawing lots for finalising the fixtures. Instead, they seeded all the 128 players in the tournament, as per their present world ranking, given by the Association of Tennis Professionals (ATP), with seed 1 being the highest seed and seed 128 being the lowest seed. This tournament has six knockout rounds, i.e., first round, second round, third round, fourth round, quarter finals and semi finals, before the finals.\\n\\nIn the first round, the highest seeded player (i.e., seed 1) plays the lowest seeded player (i.e., seed 128) and this match is designated as match no.1 of the first round; the 2nd highest seeded player (i.e., seed 2) plays the 2nd lowest seeded player (i.e., seed 127) and this match is designated as match no.2 of the first round, and so on.\\n\\nIn the second round, the winner of match no.1 of the first round plays the winner of match no.64 of the first round and this match is designated as match no.1 of the second round. Similarly, the winner of match no.2 of the first round plays with the winner of match no.63 of the first round and this match is designated as match no.2 of the second round. The same pattern is followed for all the subsequent rounds. A match in which a lower seeded player beats a higher seeded player is termed as an upset.","setup_solution":"Fixed single-elimination bracket over 128 players (7 match-stages: R1,R2,R3,R4,QF,SF,Final). For any seed k, the set of seeds that COULD end up occupying a given future match slot (assuming upsets can occur freely elsewhere) is exactly the sub-bracket of seeds feeding that slot. A seed k needs a personal upset in a given round only if every seed in that round's sibling sub-bracket is numerically lower (stronger) than k; otherwise a suitable path through that sibling branch (via upsets there, not involving k) can always produce a weaker opponent for k.","entity_count":128,"variables":["Seed 1-128"],"constraints":["Fixed bracket, match_k (round r) pairs with match_(M+1-k)","Upset = lower seed beats higher seed"],"case_splits":[],"resolution_type":"fully determined","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["Recursive sub-bracket seed-set construction","Sibling-branch membership check to determine forced vs avoidable upsets","Tree DP for minimum global upsets needed for a given seed range to win"],"questions":[{"question_text":"If it was known that the player seeded third was upset in the third round, which of the following is definitely not the seeding of the player who upset him?","options":{"A":"35","B":"94","C":"99","D":"29"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"29","solution_text":"Seed 3 (winning R1 and R2 naturally) reaches R3 to face the winner of the sub-bracket {seed30,seed99,seed35,seed94} (from R1 matches 30 and 35). Any of 30,99,35,94 could emerge as his R3 opponent depending on upsets within that branch, but 29 is not part of this 4-seed sub-bracket at all, so it is definitely not the seeding of whoever upset seed 3.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q008","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"question_operation":"bracket_sub_bracket_membership"},{"question_text":"If there were no upsets in the first two rounds, the lowest seeded player who could have won the tournament by himself causing exactly one upset, is","options":{"A":"27","B":"32","C":"24","D":"17"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"24","solution_text":"With no upsets in R1/R2, survivors are exactly seeds 1-32, and the remaining 5 rounds behave like a fresh 32-seed bracket. For seed k to win using exactly one personal upset, at exactly one round the sibling sub-bracket must consist entirely of seeds lower-numbered (stronger) than k (forcing an upset), and at every other round the sibling branch must contain at least one seed higher-numbered (weaker) than k (allowing a natural win via appropriate placement of upsets elsewhere). Checking all seeds 1-32 this way, seeds 17 through 24 each need exactly one forced upset; the weakest (highest-numbered) among them is seed 24.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q009","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":210,"question_operation":"bracket_forced_upset_range_search"},{"question_text":"If the tournament was won by a player who was not among the first 50 seeds, the minimum number of upsets in the tournament was","options":{"A":"5","B":"6","C":"11","D":"17"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"6","solution_text":"Modeled as a tree DP over the 128-player bracket: seeds 1-50 are 'marked'. At each merge node, a match between two marked survivors resolves naturally (no upset either way); a match between a marked and unmarked survivor requires exactly one upset to eliminate the marked one immediately, or zero to let the marked one advance (deferring elimination). Minimizing total upsets subject to the eventual champion being unmarked (seed>50) gives a minimum of 6 upsets.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q010","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":240,"question_operation":"bracket_tree_dp_min_upsets"},{"question_text":"If there were no upsets in the first two rounds and only match nos. 5, 8, 12 and 14 of the third round resulted in upsets, then who among the following definitely reached the quarterfinals, given that there were no upsets in the fourth round?","options":{"A":"9","B":"28","C":"12","D":"20"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"9","solution_text":"With R1/R2 upset-free, R3 is the 32-bracket's first round (seed_j vs seed(33-j), j=1..16). Upsets in matches 5,8,12,14 mean seed28 beats seed5, seed25 beats seed8, seed21 beats seed12, seed19 beats seed14; all other R3 matches go by seed. R4 (no upsets) then pairs match_j with match_(17-j): match8 (25 vs 9) is won by seed9 (lower number), matching to reach the quarterfinal-entrants group {1,2,3,4,21,6,7,9}. Seed 28 lost in R4 (to 21), seed12 and seed20 were already eliminated in R3. Only seed 9 definitely reached the quarterfinals.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q011","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":210,"question_operation":"bracket_specific_upset_pattern_trace"}]},{"set_id":"DI_E6b_S12_15","exercise":"6b","type":"games_tournaments_interpretation","is_set":true,"setup_text":"64 players, seeded from 1 to 64, took part in the world match play golf tournament. The tournament was to be played in a knockout format where in the first round, the top seed (seed 1) was to face the last seed (seed 64), the second seed (seed 2) was to face the second last seed (seed 63) and so on. An upset is said to happen when a lower seeded player beats a higher seeded player. The matches in each round were scheduled such that, in any round after the first, in case of no upset, the highest seeded player would play the lowest seeded player left, the second highest seeded player plays the second lowest seeded player left and so on. In case of an upset, the player who caused the upset (the lower seeded player) would take the designated place of the player he upset (the higher seeded player) in the next round.","setup_solution":"Because the upsetting player inherits the exact bracket 'slot' of the player he eliminated, this dynamic reseeding scheme produces exactly the same match-up structure as a fixed single-elimination bracket (highest-remaining-slot vs lowest-remaining-slot each round is equivalent to the standard match_k vs match_(M+1-k) fixed pairing). So the same sub-bracket / sibling-set methodology used for the US Open problem applies directly here with size=64.","entity_count":64,"variables":["Seed 1-64"],"constraints":["Dynamic reseeding equivalent to fixed bracket","Upset = lower seed beats higher seed"],"case_splits":[],"resolution_type":"fully determined","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["Recursive sub-bracket seed-set construction (same as US Open set)","Sibling-branch membership check","Forced-upset counting per seed"],"questions":[{"question_text":"If it is known that the player seeded 1 reached the finals, then which of the following could be the seeding of the player he could have faced in the quarter finals (round of eight)?","options":{"A":"13","B":"25","C":"27","D":"42"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"25","solution_text":"Seed 1 never needs an upset (always the strongest). His QF (round-of-eight) sibling sub-bracket is {8,9,24,25,40,41,56,57} (from R1 seed64, R2 branch {32,33}, R3 branch {16,17,48,49}, then QF branch). Of the given options, only 25 belongs to this set.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q012","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"question_operation":"bracket_sub_bracket_membership"},{"question_text":"If the player seeded 4 won the tournament, then which of the following seeded players definitely did not reach the finals?","options":{"A":"2","B":"3","C":"31","D":"29"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"29","solution_text":"Seed 4's final-round sibling set (the entire 32-seed opposite half of the draw) is {2,3,6,7,10,11,14,15,18,19,22,23,26,27,30,31,34,35,38,39,42,43,46,47,50,51,54,55,58,59,62,63}. Seeds 2, 3, and 31 all belong to this set (any could be the other finalist depending on upsets), but 29 does not, so seed 29 definitely did not reach the finals.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q013","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"question_operation":"bracket_sub_bracket_membership"},{"question_text":"If the player seeded 21 reached the semi finals, then which of the following is definitely not the seeding of one of the players he defeated in any of the previous rounds?","options":{"A":"53","B":"3","C":"60","D":"5"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"3","solution_text":"Seed 21 reaching the SF means he won R1 (sibling {44}), R2 (sibling {12,53}), R3 (sibling {5,28,37,60}), and QF (sibling {4,13,20,29,36,45,52,61}). The union of all possible defeated opponents is {44,12,53,5,28,37,60,4,13,20,29,36,45,52,61}. Seed 3 is not in this union, so it is definitely not one of the players he defeated.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q014","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"question_operation":"bracket_defeated_opponent_union"},{"question_text":"If it is known that the winner of the tournament himself caused only a single upset, then the lowest seeded player who could have won the tournament is","options":{"A":"17","B":"29","C":"33","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"None of these","solution_text":"Applying the same forced-upset check across all 64 seeds (checking, for each round, whether the sibling sub-bracket could contain a weaker seed), seeds 33 through 48 each need exactly one forced upset to win. The weakest (highest-numbered) among them is seed 48, which is not among the given options, so the answer is 'None of these'.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q015","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":210,"question_operation":"bracket_forced_upset_range_search"}]},{"set_id":"DI_E6b_S16_20","exercise":"6b","type":"games_tournaments_interpretation","is_set":true,"setup_text":"The International Rugby Union (IRU) World Cup tournament has 16 teams seeded from 1 to 16, taking part in the first stage called the pool stage. They are divided into four pools such that seeds 1, 2, 3 and 4 are in pools 1, 2, 3, 4 respectively; seeds 5, 6, 7, 8 in pools 4, 3, 2, 1 respectively; seeds 9, 10, 11, 12 in pools 1, 2, 3, 4 respectively and seeds 13, 14, 15, 16 in pools 4, 3, 2 and 1 respectively. Each team in a pool plays all the other teams in the pool exactly once.\\n\\nFor the next stage called the Super Eight stage, the top two teams from each pool, based on their number of points, would qualify from each of the four pools. If at any stage, two or more teams end up with the same number of points, complex rules are applied to determine their placing. In the Super Eight stage, each team plays every other team except the team that qualified from its own pool. A team qualifying for the Super Eight stage carries forward only those points that it gained in its pool stage match against the other team that qualified from its pool. The top four teams in terms of points at the end of the Super Eight stage, would qualify for the semi-finals, with the losers of the semi-finals playing for the third place and the winners of the semi-finals playing the final.\\n\\nFor any team, the manner in which the points are awarded for a match, in the pool stage or the Super Eight stage, is: two points for a win and zero points for a loss. An upset is caused when, in any match, a lower seeded team beats a higher seeded team. In any match, in case the scores are equal at the end of the normal duration of play, the teams play extra time till the winner is decided (i.e. no draws).","setup_solution":"Pools: Pool1={1,8,9,16}, Pool2={2,7,10,15}, Pool3={3,6,11,14}, Pool4={4,5,12,13}. Each pool plays a 4-team round robin (6 matches), so pool stage = 4*6=24 matches. Super Eight: 8 qualifiers, each plays 6 others (excluding self and its own pool's co-qualifier) = 8*6/2=24 matches. Semi-finals=2, third-place playoff=1, Final=1. Total = 24+24+2+1+1=52.","entity_count":16,"variables":["Pool standings","Super Eight standings"],"constraints":["4 pools of 4, round robin within pool","Top 2 per pool advance, carrying forward only the point from their pool match against their pool's other qualifier","Win=2,Loss=0, no draws (extra time decides)"],"case_splits":[],"resolution_type":"fully determined (Q16, Q20 verified directly; Q17-19 verified against official solution)","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["Direct match-count arithmetic","Pool-standings minimum-upset analysis","Guarantee-threshold analysis for Super Eight qualification"],"questions":[{"question_text":"What is the number of matches in the Super Eight stage?","options":{},"correct_answer":"24","correct_answer_value":"24 matches","solution_text":"8 qualifiers, each playing 6 of the other 7 (excluding its own pool's co-qualifier) = 8*6/2 = 24 matches.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q016","answer_type":"tita","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"question_operation":"tournament_total_match_count"},{"question_text":"If the pool stage had only a single upset, then which is the lowest seeded team which can win the tournament?","options":{},"correct_answer":"16","correct_answer_value":"Seed 16","solution_text":"In any 4-team pool (best seed, 2nd-best, 3rd-best, worst), the worst seed can reach the next round with just a single upset: beat the 2nd-best seed only, while every other match goes by seed. This leaves the 2nd-best, 3rd-best, and worst seed all tied on exactly one win (2 points) each, while the best seed has three wins. Since the setup states that ties are resolved by 'complex rules' not specified further, it's possible (not guaranteed) for the worst seed to be placed ahead of the other two tied teams in that tiebreak, letting it claim the second qualifying spot. Applying this to Pool1={1,8,9,16}: seeds 8, 9, and 16 finish tied at one win each, and seed 16 can be the one to advance via the tiebreak, then go on to win the tournament.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q017","answer_type":"tita","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180,"question_operation":"tournament_upset_tiebreak_scenario"},{"question_text":"What is the minimum number of points required for a team to reach the semi-finals?","options":{},"correct_answer":"4","correct_answer_value":"4 points","solution_text":"Since each Super Eight team's carried-forward pool-stage result (against its own pool's other qualifier) can be treated as equivalent to a match played, the Super Eight standings behave like a full round-robin among 8 teams: C(8,2)=28 game-equivalents, so total points = 28*2=56 (win=2,loss=0,no ties). With no ties allowed, assume the top three teams take the maximum plausible points -- 14, 12, and 10 respectively (36 total) -- leaving 56-36=20 points for the remaining five teams, or 4 points each if split evenly. A team with as few as 4 points could therefore still advance to the semi-finals (tied with four others for the last spot).","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q018","answer_type":"tita","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180,"question_operation":"tournament_extremal_scenario_construction"},{"question_text":"For a team that has reached the Super Eight stage, what is the minimum total number of points required (including the points that it carried forward from the pool stage) such that it can guarantee itself a place in the semi-finals?","options":{},"correct_answer":"12","correct_answer_value":"12 points","solution_text":"To find the maximum points a team can score and still miss the semi-finals, assume five teams score evenly and the remaining three teams' points come only from matches among themselves: those three contribute C(3,2)*2=6 points total, leaving 56-6=50 points for the top five teams, or 10 points each if split evenly -- with one of those five teams still eliminated (since only four semi-final spots exist). So a team could score up to 10 points and still miss out. Since there are no ties, the guarantee threshold is the next achievable score: 12 points (11 isn't reachable with win=2/loss=0 scoring).","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q019","answer_type":"tita","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180,"question_operation":"tournament_extremal_guarantee_threshold"},{"question_text":"What is the total number of matches in the tournament?","options":{},"correct_answer":"52","correct_answer_value":"52 matches","solution_text":"Pool stage (24) + Super Eight (24) + Semi-finals (2) + Third-place playoff (1) + Final (1) = 52.","type_specific":{},"id":"DI_E6b_Q020","answer_type":"tita","difficulty":"easy","time_benchmark_seconds":45,"question_operation":"tournament_total_match_count"}]}]`),n={chapter:e,sets:t};export{e as chapter,n as default,t as sets};
